<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/intermediate/criminal-vs-civil-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every legal dispute sits on one of two tracks. <strong>Criminal law</strong> punishes offences
    against society — the state prosecutes, and liberty is at stake, so proof must be near
    certain. <strong>Civil law</strong> settles disputes between private parties — money and
    obligations are at stake, so "more likely than not" suffices. Same event, different courts,
    different standards, different consequences.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four structural differences — with the famous case that shows them all" widget="equationStep" config={ccCfg} />

  <h2>The vocabulary this distinction generates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plaintiff vs prosecutor:</strong> who initiates. <strong>Defendant:</strong> who answers, in both.</li>
    <li><strong>Crimes need mens rea + actus reus</strong> (guilty mind + guilty act) — most criminal defences attack one of the two (insanity, duress, self-defence).</li>
    <li><strong>Torts live on the civil side:</strong> negligence, defamation, nuisance — wrongs against individuals, compensated in damages (the torts lesson on this ladder).</li>
    <li><strong>Settlement dominates civil law:</strong> ~95% of civil cases settle before trial — trials are the exception both systems are built to avoid.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> standard first-year criminal and civil procedure texts; Glanville Williams, Learning the Law.</p>
</TopicPage>
